1. Flake.nix:
```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixos-20.09";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }: {
    packages = let
      pkgs = import nixpkgs {};
      my-lib = pkgs.buildPackages.stdenv.mkDerivation rec {
        name = "my-lib";
        src = ./.;
        buildInputs = [ pkgs.clang ];
        installPhase = ''
          mkdir -p $out/lib
          clang -shared -o $out/lib/my-lib.so my-lib.c
        '';
      };
      my-tool = pkgs.buildPackages.stdenv.mkDerivation rec {
        name = "my-tool";
        src = ./.;
        buildInputs = [ my-lib ];
        installPhase = ''
          mkdir -p $out/bin
          clang -o $out/bin/my-tool main.c -L$my-lib/lib -lmy-lib
        '';
      };
    in { inherit (pkgs) my-lib my-tool; };

    devShell = pkgs.buildPackages.stdenv.mkDerivation rec {
      name = "dev-shell";
      src = ./.;
      buildInputs = with pkgs; [ clang cargo ];
      shellHook = ''
        export RUST_SRC_PATH="${pkgs.rustPlatform.buildRustPackage { src = ./src; }}/lib/rustlib/src"
      '';
    };
  };
}
```

Nix's lazy evaluation interacts with fixed-output derivations in the following ways:

* Evaluation of a Nix expression is done lazily, meaning that only the minimum amount of computation necessary to produce the output of a derivation is performed. This makes Nix evaluation very efficient and allows for incremental builds.
* When using fixed-output derivations (such as `mkDerivation` in this case), Nix will cache the output of the derivation, meaning that if the input to the derivation has not changed, the output will be reused from the cache instead of being rebuilt. This can significantly speed up the build process and make it more efficient.
* However, because Nix is lazy, if a fixed-output derivation depends on an input that has changed, the entire output of the derivation will need to be rebuilt, regardless of whether any other inputs have also changed. This means that if any other inputs to the derivation have changed, they must be rebuilt as well, which can lead to inefficiencies and unnecessary rebuilds.
* To mitigate this issue, it is generally a good practice to use `nix-shell` to enter a development environment for the package being built, where all of the inputs are evaluated eagerly instead of lazily. This allows for more efficient building and reduces the risk of unnecessary rebuilds.
2. The scoping error in the Nix expression is due to the fact that the `lib` variable is defined inside a let binding, but it is used outside of it. To fix this, the `lib` variable should be moved outside of the let binding so that it can be used in both places.
3. To expose a formatter output (packages.formatter) that runs alejandra on all .nix files, we can use the following flake:
```nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixos-20.09";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }: {
    packages = let
      pkgs = import nixpkgs {};
      my-lib = pkgs.buildPackages.stdenv.mkDerivation rec {
        name = "my-lib";
        src = ./.;
        buildInputs = [ pkgs.clang ];
        installPhase = ''
          mkdir -p $out/lib
          clang -shared -o $out/lib/my-lib.so my-lib.c
        '';
      };
      my-tool = pkgs.buildPackages.stdenv.mkDerivation rec {
        name = "my-tool";
        src = ./.;
        buildInputs = [ my-lib ];
        installPhase = ''
          mkdir -p $out/bin
          clang -o $out/bin/my-tool main.c -L$my-lib/lib -lmy-lib
        '';
      };
    in { inherit (pkgs) my-lib my-tool; };

    devShell = pkgs.buildPackages.stdenv.mkDerivation rec {
      name = "dev-shell";
      src = ./.;
      buildInputs = with pkgs; [ clang cargo ];
      shellHook = ''
        export RUST_SRC_PATH="${pkgs.rustPlatform.buildRustPackage { src = ./src; }}/lib/rustlib/src"
      '';
    };

    formatter = pkgs.writeShellScriptBin "formatter" ''
      # Expose the alejandra command as a shell script so that it can be used in other derivations
      export PATH="${pkgs.alejandra}/bin:$PATH"
      # Run alejandra on all .nix files
      alejandra **/*.nix
    '';
  };
}
```
In this flake, the `formatter` output is defined as a shell script that uses the `alejandra` command to format all .nix files in the package directory. This output can then be used in other derivations to ensure consistency across the package.
4. If nixpkgs only provided binary caches and no source, it would not be possible to rebuild the package from scratch because the sources for the packages are not available. In this case, the only option would be to use a pre-built version of the package that has been built with a fixed input set, which may or may not have the desired behavior. To ensure reproducibility in this situation, it is important to provide both binary caches and source for all packages. This ensures that the package can be rebuilt from scratch if necessary, while still providing a way to use pre-built versions of the package if they are available.

