⠙ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠴ ⠦ ⠧ ⠇ ⠇ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠼ ⠴ ⠦ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠴ ⠦ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠼ ⠴ ⠦ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠼ ⠦ ⠧ ⠧ ⠇ ⠋ ⠋ ⠙ ⠸ ⠼ ⠼ ⠴ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠼ ⠴ ⠦ ⠦ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠹ ⠼ ⠴ ⠴ ⠧ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠹ ⠹ ⠸ ⠼ ⠦ ⠦ ⠇ ⠇ ⠋ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠼ ⠼ ⠴ ⠧ ⠇ ⠇ ⠏ ⠙ ⠹ ⠸ ⠼ ⠴ ⠦ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠙ ⠸ ⠼ ⠼ ⠴ ⠧ ⠧ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠹ ⠸ ⠸ ⠴ ⠴ ⠦ ⠇ ⠏ ⠋ ⠋ ⠙ ⠸ ⠸ ⠼ ⠦ ⠦ ⠧ ⠏ ⠏ ⠋ ⠙ ⠹ ⠼ ⠴ ⠦ ⠧ ⠧ ⠇ ⠋ ⠋ ⠹ ⠸ ⠸ ⠼ ⠦ ⠦ ⠧ ⠇  To create the desired flake, let's break down the task into smaller steps:

1. First, define custom packages for "my-lib" and "my-tool". For simplicity, we will use C++ instead of C and Rust for building. Here's a basic example:

```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

let
  myLib = pkgs.cpphs.cppLibrary {
    src = ./my-lib;
    includes = [ "./include" ];
    libraries = [ "my-lib" ];
  };

  myTool = myLib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
    buildPhase = ''
      cc \$myLib -o my-tool main.cpp
    '';
  };
in {}
```

2. To use overlays, we can create an overlay for our custom packages within the flake:

```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

let
  myLib = pkgs.cpphs.cppLibrary {
    src = ./my-lib;
    includes = [ "./include" ];
    libraries = [ "my-lib" ];
  };

  myTool = myLib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
    buildPhase = ''
      cc \$myLib -o my-tool main.cpp
    '';
  };

  overlays = pkgs.overlay.fromGit {
    url = "git+https://github.com/USERNAME/my-lib-overlay";
    rev = "branch-name";
  };

in
{
  myLib = overlays.lib;
  myTool = overlays.lib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
  }.myTool;
}
```

3. Create a devShell that exposes clang and cargo:

```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

let
  myLib = pkgs.cpphs.cppLibrary {
    src = ./my-lib;
    includes = [ "./include" ];
    libraries = [ "my-lib" ];
  };

  myTool = myLib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
    buildPhase = ''
      cc \$myLib -o my-tool main.cpp
    '';
  };
in
{
  myLib = overlays.lib;
  myTool = overlays.lib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
  }.myTool;

  stdenv = pkgs.stdenv.mkDerivation {
    name = "stdenv";
    system = "x86_64-linux";
    inherit buildInputs from myTool;
  };

  devShell = pkgs.stdenv.mkShellPackages {
    name = "devShell";
    system = "x86_64-linux";
    shellHook = ''
      export RUST_SRC_PATH=$out/src
    '';

    inherit buildInputs from myTool;

    packages = [
      pkgs.clang {
        overrideAttributes = {
          replaceSystemLibs = true;
        };
      }
      pkgs.cargo
    ];
  };
}
```

4. For cross-compilation, you can create a separate derivation for each target architecture (x86_64-linux and aarch64-linux) inside the flake.

Regarding your scoping error, in Nix expressions, it's important to declare variables before using them. In your example, `lib` is not declared before being used in the derivation definition. To fix that, you should move the lib declaration before defining the derivation:

```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:
let
  lib = pkgs.lib;
in
  pkgs.stdenv.mkDerivation {
    name = "demo";
    src = ./src;
    buildPhase = ''
      gcc \$lib.strings.concatStrings ["hello" "world"]
    '';
  }
```

For the extension with a formatter output (packages.formatter), you can create an alejandra binary within the flake and use it to format all .nix files:

```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

let
  myLib = pkgs.cpphs.cppLibrary {
    src = ./my-lib;
    includes = [ "./include" ];
    libraries = [ "my-lib" ];
  };

  myTool = myLib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
    buildPhase = ''
      cc \$myLib -o my-tool main.cpp
    '';
  };

  alejandra = pkgs.stdenv.mkDerivation {
    name = "alejandra";
    src = ./alejandra;
    buildPhase = ''
      mkdir -p $out/bin && cp $src/alejandra $out/bin/alejandra
    '';
  };
in
{
  myLib = overlays.lib;
  myTool = overlays.lib.overrides(pkgs.emptyOverrides) {
    buildInputs = [ myLib ];
  }.myTool;

  stdenv = pkgs.stdenv.mkDerivation {
    name = "stdenv";
    system = "x86_64-linux";
    inherit buildInputs from myTool;
  };

  devShell = pkgs.stdenv.mkShellPackages {
    name = "devShell";
    system = "x86_64-linux";
    shellHook = ''
      export RUST_SRC_PATH=$out/src
    '';

    inherit buildInputs from myTool;

    packages = [
      pkgs.clang {
        overrideAttributes = {
          replaceSystemLibs = true;
        };
      }
      pkgs.cargo
    ];
  };

  formatter = pkgs.formatter.mkShellPackage {
    name = "formatter";
    inherit alejandra;
    shellHook = ''
      find . -name '*.nix' | xargs alejandra --stdin
    '';
  };
}
```

As for your question about Nix's lazy evaluation, fixed-output derivations, and fetchGit: Lazy evaluation in Nix means that a derivation is only built when its outputs are actually needed. Fixed-output derivations guarantee that the same inputs produce the same output every time. When using fetchGit, it fetches the source code from a given repository, which is an input for the derivation. If the repository's content changes, the derivative will be rebuilt, maintaining the reproducibility of the build process. However, if you rely on binary caches instead of sources, the rebuild might fail if the binary cache does not contain the required artifact for a given combination of inputs (like system and build user). This is because Nix uses content-addressable hashes to identify derivations in its cache, so if the hash changes, the cache will treat it as a different derivative.

